{
  "name": "How to Prepare Your Home for Sale or Rent After Damage",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Getting your home ready for sale or rent after damage can feel like a daunting task, but a little planning goes a long way. First things first: inspect your property early and thoroughly. Take lots of photos and notes of any defects you find. This documentation is your best friend. Next, sort out all those findings by severity. You'll want to prioritize things that impact safety, structural integrity, and essential systems. These are the non-negotiables that buyers or renters will definitely notice, and they’re often legally important too. Once you've got your list, get contractor estimates for those high-impact repairs. This helps you understand the cost and decide whether it makes more sense to fix them, offer a repair credit, or simply disclose the issue. Remember, cosmetic wear is often a lower priority, but active leaks or safety hazards need immediate attention. For bigger issues like roof damage, foundation problems, or electrical hazards, it's really important to get licensed contractors involved. Don't get caught overpaying for minor cosmetic upgrades that don't add much value. Focus on what truly matters to a potential buyer or renter, and what's required for disclosure. HH Remodeling LLC can help you with these repairs, making your home market-ready.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
